[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"API Design Paradigms: REST, GraphQL, gRPC, and WebSockets"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right API design paradigm is one of the most critical architectural decisions in system design. Different communication styles cater to different latency requirements, payload sizes, and client-server interaction models. This tutorial explores the four primary API paradigms, their use cases, and trade-offs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. REST (Representational State Transfer)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"REST is an architectural style based on standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and resource-oriented URLs. It relies heavily on statelessness and uniform interfaces, making it the industry standard for public-facing web APIs."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Highly cacheable, human-readable, widely understood, leverages existing HTTP infrastructure."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Prone to over-fetching and under-fetching data, requires multiple round-trips for complex object graphs."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"GET /api/v1/users/123 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Accept: application/json"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. GraphQL"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Developed by Meta, GraphQL is a query language for APIs that gives clients the power to request exactly what they need and nothing more. A single endpoint processes queries, mutations, and subscriptions, resolving data through a strongly typed schema."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Eliminates over-fetching and under-fetching, strong typing, excellent for complex front-end applications."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Complex caching mechanisms, potential for expensive nested queries (DoS vulnerability), steep learning curve."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"query {
  user(id: "123") {
    name
    email
    posts(limit: 5) {
      title
    }
  }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. gRPC (Google Remote Procedure Call)"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"gRPC is a high-performance, open-source universal RPC framework. It uses HTTP/2 for transport, Protocol Buffers (protobufs) as the interface definition language and serialization format, and supports bidirectional streaming natively."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Extremely fast serialization/deserialization, compact binary payloads, strong contract enforcement via proto files, built-in streaming."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Not human-readable without tools, requires specialized browser support (gRPC-Web), less suitable for open public APIs."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"syntax = "proto3";

service UserService {
  rpc GetUser (UserRequest) returns (UserResponse);
}

message UserRequest {
  string id = 1;
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"4. WebSockets"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"WebSockets provide full-duplex communication channels over a single, long-lived TCP connection. Unlike REST or GraphQL where the client must poll for updates, WebSockets allow the server to push data to the client instantly."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Real-time, low-latency updates, minimal overhead after the initial HTTP handshake."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Stateful connections complicate horizontal scaling, requires specialized load balancing, drops connections on unstable networks."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"const socket = new WebSocket("wss://stream.example.com");
socket.onmessage = function(event) {
  console.log("Received data: " + event.data);
};"},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Selection Matrix"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use REST for standard CRUD operations, public-facing developer APIs, and easily cacheable web resources."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use GraphQL when optimizing mobile bandwidth, building rich client-driven apps, or aggregating data from multiple microservices."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use gRPC for high-throughput, low-latency microservice-to-microservice communication internal to your infrastructure."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use WebSockets for real-time dashboards, chat applications, collaborative editing tools, and live notification systems."}]
